# Search autocomplete

The publishing search autocomplete suggests queries for a large search service. The guide distinguishes it from a static component-name autocomplete. [Official publishing guidance](https://design-guide.publishing.service.gov.uk/components/search-autocomplete/).

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation; they do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## When to use it

A small documentation catalogue usually needs a labelled search or filter rather than predicted queries. For HA entities, prefer the native entity picker so permissions, names and keyboard behaviour remain consistent. Suggestions must be derived from the permitted local dataset, never invented entity IDs.

## Structure and behaviour

The local examples use a labelled native input with a datalist populated from the same fixed records shown below it. Browser-native suggestions reduce typing; a submitted query filters the visible records by their title and context. People can type a different query, open a record, inspect a no-match result and clear the filters. A polite status reports the result count without moving focus. Do not add conflicting custom combobox roles to the native control.

## Home Assistant boundary

These are working local autocomplete and search specimens, not the government’s predictive query model. The browser controls how datalist suggestions are presented; their keyboard and assistive-technology behaviour needs testing in supported browsers. Filtering uses a small, fixed dataset and does not query HA. No cloud model, query logging, storage or external search API is connected. Without JavaScript, all records and native details remain readable and submit buttons stay disabled.

